<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sabratha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sabratha was an ancient city on the coast of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, founded as a Phoenician trading post and later one of the three Emporia that gave Tripolitania its name. It prospered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the export of goods brought by Saharan caravans, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the late 2nd century CE is one of the best preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The site is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabratha.jpg" alt="Sabratha" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theatre_of_Sabratha,_Libya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sabratha lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, in the Zawiya District, about 70 kilometres west of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>. It was the westernmost of the three cities of Roman Tripolis, alongside Oea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a>. Greek writers called it Sabrata, Sabaratha, Sabratha and also Abrotonon. The Phoenician name Tsabratan seems to be a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> one, which suggests a native settlement existed before the trading post.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The port was established, perhaps about 500 BCE, as the Phoenician trading post of Tsabratan, an outlet for the products of the African hinterland. After the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> it fell within the sphere of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> it became part of the short-lived Numidian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Massinissa</a>, which was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> as the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa-nova">Africa Nova</a> in the 1st century BCE. The city was romanised and rebuilt in the 2nd and 3rd centuries CE, and reached its monumental peak under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severan-dynasty">Severan dynasty</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, who was born at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a>, expanding to nearly twice its former size.</p><p>Earthquakes badly damaged it in the 4th century, particularly that of 365, and under the Vandal kingdom in the 5th century large parts of the city were abandoned. It enjoyed a small revival under Byzantine rule, when churches and a defensive wall enclosing only a small part of the city were built, and it was the seat of a bishopric. Within a hundred years of the Muslim invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> trade had shifted to other ports and Sabratha dwindled to a village.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> keeps its three-storey architectural backdrop, and the site has temples of Liber Pater, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapis">Serapis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, a Christian basilica of the time of Justinian, and mosaic floors, best preserved in the seaward baths and the black and white floors of the theatre baths. In 186 CE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a> dedicated a temple to Hercules and to his own father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>; a wall painting in it showed Commodus carried to heaven on an eagle.</p><p>Italian archaeologists have excavated the site since 1921, and a British team directed by Kathleen Kenyon and John Ward-Perkins worked there between 1948 and 1951. The site was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1982. Its coast is eroding, and its building stone, calcarenite, weathers easily, so that the baths, the olive press and the harbour have suffered most.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sabratha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Gaiseric's death the kingdom lost ground to the Berbers: the Kingdom of Altava, Masties's kingdom of the Aurès in 483–484, Guenfan's Frexenses south of Thugga in 510 and Cabaon's Laguatan, who took Oea and <b>Sabratha</b> and defeated Thrasamund's expedition of 523.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Rome’s rule kept their own cults, while new foundations by Roman citizens and their allies followed Roman models: newly municipal <b>Sabratha</b> built a Capitolium, and by the middle of the 1st century CE Gaulish Vertault had given up its sacrifice of horses and dogs for a Romanised cult.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>With Oea and <b>Sabratha</b> it formed the three Emporia that later gave Tripolitania its name, linking Mediterranean shipping with caravan routes across the Sahara.</blockquote><a href="/state/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 303 Diocletian separated the eastern part of Africa Proconsularis to form the province of Tripolitania, the 'land of three cities': Leptis Magna, Oea (Tripoli) and <b>Sabratha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a></li></ul></section>
